Resumen

Introduced authorization flaw: newly added contact package mutation endpoints use tenant_ops_router instead of an admin/manager-only router, while accepting sensitive payment and status fields from the caller.

The commit adds treatment package management. Catalog CRUD is admin-only, but contact package assignment, patch, and refund are registered on tenant_ops_router, which only requires the 'agent' role. The request schemas let callers set payment_status to 'paid' and patch payment/status fields. As a result, any authenticated agent for a tenant can directly POST a package assignment with payment_status='paid' and optionally payment_amount=0, causing the booking flow to treat it as an active paid package with the full session balance. The same agent can PATCH package payment/status metadata or DELETE/refund a customer's active package, causing financial/entitlement manipulation and denial of purchased sessions. These are administrative/payment operations and should require at least admin/manager authorization or server-side payment-provider confirmation for paid/refunded transitions.

Validación

 Confirm the new contact package mutation routes are registered under an agent-accessible router rather than an admin/manager-only router.
 Confirm request schemas accept sensitive payment and package status fields, including paid/refunded transitions and zero payment amount.
 Confirm the POST path persists caller-controlled payment_status/payment_amount and grants the full session balance.
 Confirm PATCH and DELETE/refund paths are reachable by an agent and can alter payment/status/remaining sessions.
 Confirm the booking flow later treats active paid packages with remaining sessions as usable customer entitlements.
