async def _list_active_contact_packages(
The booking flow automatically offers packages whose payment_status is 'paid', so an agent-created paid package becomes usable for session consumption.
    conn: asyncpg.Connection,
    tenant_id: UUID,
    contact_id: UUID,
    service_id: UUID,
) -> list[dict[str, Any]]:
    """TASK-0051: return active, paid, non-expired packages of the contact whose
    catalogue covers the picked service (an empty ``includes_service_ids`` means
    the package applies to any service).
    """
    rows = await conn.fetch(
        """
        select cp.id, cp.package_id, cp.remaining_sessions, cp.expires_at,
               tp.name as package_name, tp.includes_service_ids
        from app.contact_packages cp
        join app.treatment_packages tp
          on tp.id = cp.package_id and tp.tenant_id = cp.tenant_id
        where cp.tenant_id=$1
          and cp.contact_id=$2
          and cp.status='active'
          and cp.payment_status='paid'
          and cp.remaining_sessions > 0
          and (cp.expires_at is null or cp.expires_at > now())
          and (
            coalesce(array_length(tp.includes_service_ids, 1), 0) = 0
            or $3 = any(tp.includes_service_ids)
          )
        order by cp.expires_at asc nulls last, cp.purchased_at asc
        limit 5
        """,
        tenant_id,
        contact_id,
        service_id,
    )
    return [dict(row) for row in rows]
Análisis de la ruta de ataque

The original high severity is appropriate. Evidence shows the vulnerable code is in the main runtime API, is reachable over authenticated tenant operations routes, and was dynamically exercised with an agent-role TestClient PoC. The authorization gap allows payment/refund/entitlement manipulation that should be manager/admin or payment-provider controlled. It is not raised to critical because exploitation requires an authenticated same-tenant agent and the evidence does not show cross-tenant access, platform compromise, credential exposure, or code execution.

Ruta
Agent-authenticated HTTP client --Authenticated agent passes require_min_role('agent')--> FastAPI /v1 tenant operations router --Routes registered on tenant_ops_router--> Contact package POST/PATCH/DELETE handlers --Caller-controlled paid/refunded/status fields are inserted or updated--> app.contact_packages payment_status/status/remaining_sessions --Booking selects status='active' and payment_status='paid' with remaining sessions--> Booking flow consumes active paid package sessions
This is a real, reachable authorization flaw in the main FastAPI product API. The contact package mutation endpoints are included under /v1 and registered on tenant_ops_router, which requires only the agent role. Their request models allow caller-controlled payment and status fields, including paid and refunded. The POST handler stores payload.payment_status and seeds full remaining_sessions; PATCH changes payment/status fields; DELETE marks packages refunded and zeroes sessions. The booking flow later treats active, paid packages with remaining_sessions > 0 as usable entitlements. The provided validation PoC further confirmed that a viewer was rejected but an agent could create a paid package with payment_amount 0, patch status/payment metadata, and trigger refund SQL. Severity remains high because this is unauthorized same-tenant financial/entitlement manipulation by a low-privileged actor, but not critical because it requires authentication and no cross-tenant or platform-wide compromise is evidenced.

Probabilidad
High - The attack is a straightforward remote API call by any authenticated agent. Package and contact package IDs are exposed through agent-level list endpoints, and the validation PoC demonstrated exploitation through normal FastAPI routing with auth active. | Remote network vector
Impacto
High - A low-privileged tenant agent can alter financially meaningful package payment and entitlement state: create paid sessions without verified payment, modify payment/status metadata, or refund and zero a customer's package. This is a significant integrity and business-logic impact within a tenant, though no cross-tenant compromise or full account takeover is shown.
Suposiciones
The FastAPI application in app/main.py and app/api/v1/routes.py is part of the deployed product API.
Tenant users with the agent role are realistic in-scope actors and are not trusted to perform admin/payment operations.
Treatment packages and contacts in the same tenant are discoverable or otherwise known to agents through normal tenant operations endpoints.
Paid/refunded treatment package state is financially and operationally significant for this application.
Authenticated tenant user with at least the agent role
A valid same-tenant contact_id
A valid same-tenant active treatment package id for assignment, or existing contact_package_id for update/refund
Controles
authenticate_request is present
tenant_ops_router uses require_min_role('agent', allow_service=True)
tenant_id_from_request and tenant-scoped SQL constrain the issue to the selected tenant
Audit calls are present for assigned/updated/refunded actions but do not prevent exploitation
Catalog CRUD routes use tenant_admin_router, showing stronger authorization exists but was not applied to contact package payment/refund mutations
Puntos ciegos
Static repository review cannot confirm the actual production ingress, load balancer exposure, or whether all tenants use the package feature.
The precise business policy for what an agent is allowed to do is not fully specified, though the code itself distinguishes admin-only catalog CRUD from agent-level operations.
No live production database or cloud deployment was inspected; reachability is inferred from routes, Docker configuration, and provided validation PoC.
Financial settlement side effects of marking refunded are not fully visible in the inspected code, so impact is based on application entitlement state and booking behavior.